<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: Rebels against Empire</h1><p class="ws-lede">Six risings against the greatest powers of their age.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/rebels-against-empire">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Their pages on the path will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Vercingetorix</li><li>Túpac Amaru II</li><li>Toussaint Louverture</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1743–1803</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>1738–1781</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 82–46 BCE</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Spartacus</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;The gladiator whose army of the enslaved shook Rome for two years.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 4, <b>Boudica</b>: &#8220;The queen whose revolt burned three Roman towns before it broke.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 6, <b>Toussaint Louverture</b>: &#8220;The general of the enslaved who defeated three empires and made Haiti possible.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Six risings against the greatest powers of their age.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/rebels-against-empire. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: Rebels against Empire</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Vercingetorix <span>3: c. 82–46 BCE</span></li><li>Túpac Amaru II <span>2: 1738–1781</span></li><li>Toussaint Louverture <span>1: 1743–1803</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
